<Project Sdk="BHS.Revit.Sdk/1.5.8">

    <!--
      The probe's stand-in for a feature's declaration assembly: a module the probe lists, and the
      availability rule its Entry button asks.

      It exists so that the Entry arrangement is measured before a feature depends on it. A feature's
      buttons now live in <P>.Entry, the rule in <P>.Declaration and the command in <P>.Feature, and
      three things about that arrangement cannot be read from metadata: whether Revit accepts an
      availability class whose logic sits in a generic base in another assembly, whether the feature
      assembly stays out of the AppDomain while that class is being asked, and whether a command found
      by its feature reaches the host that declared it. The probe answers them in a sweep with the tab
      shown - BHS_PROBE_SHOW_TAB=1, with a person at the screen.

      The same rules as a real declaration, and RefCheck holds it to them by name: this assembly ends
      in .Declaration, so RVTDEC001 refuses any reference to .Feature or .Entry, and RVTDEC002 any to
      RevitAPIUI. BHS.Logging is reached through BHS.Revit.Abstractions, which already references it;
      it is on the allowed list either way.

      Unlike a real rule, GateRule keeps counters. They are what the probe reads, and a probe rule is
      the one place the owner allowed them - never an Entry class.
    -->

    <PropertyGroup>
        <TargetFrameworks>net48-revit2024;net8.0-revit2025;net8.0-revit2026;net10.0-revit2027</TargetFrameworks>
        <RootNamespace>BHS.Revit.Probe.Declaration</RootNamespace>
        <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <ProjectReference Include="..\BHS.Revit.Abstractions\BHS.Revit.Abstractions.csproj"/>
    </ItemGroup>

</Project>
